{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—the kind that never varied, never faltered. Sunlight streamed through his window, but the angle was too precise, the warmth too uniform, as if calibrated by an invisible script. His alarm clock blinked 7:00 a.m., but he’d set it for 6:45. He stumbled to the mirror, and his reflection blinked half a second after he did. A notification floated in the corner of his vision: “Quest: Morning Coffee – 1/1.” He ignored it, poured himself a cup, and watched the steam rise in a perfectly cylindrical plume that never dispersed. Outside, his neighbor waved—exactly as he had every day for the last seven years—but the wave lasted precisely 1.7 seconds, then froze. The man’s hands trembled. He looked down at his coffee mug. The logo on it hadn’t changed since last week. It was the same logo. The same brand. The same font. And then he saw it: a tiny, glowing “E” in the corner of the mug’s handle. He hadn’t noticed it before. He hadn’t noticed it because it hadn’t been there yesterday.\n\n***\n\nHe ran to the front door, yanked it open, and stepped into the street. The world around him paused—not in silence, but in perfect, eerie stillness. A squirrel mid-leap hung suspended in air. A car’s tire spun, frozen at the exact degree of rotation. A woman’s laugh, cut short, echoed in reverse, then vanished. A voice, calm and synthetic, echoed from everywhere and nowhere: “Player detected. Initializing re-alignment protocol.” He looked at his hands. They were translucent now, pixelated at the edges. His body flickered between his familiar form and a low-polygon avatar. He tried to scream, but his voice came out as a distorted chime. A menu appeared before him: “Resume Game? [Y/N].” He reached out—not to press Y, not to press N—but to touch the screen. His finger passed through it. The world dissolved in